 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-ticks-are-its-own-work"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's ruling, 2026-11-15: \"disable the tick counting test until we can reliably measure ticks.\" Measured [M, root, target/results/full-run.edn at 7cf0c01b64]: main's full run was red on 8 tests at the 300M ceiling (:seon.config.agent/test-ticks, lowered from 10G by tests-4 from a percentile measurement, not arithmetic), and one digest held both a 161M pass and a 300M red (seon.ctx-test/a-swap-costs-what-it-changed at ab7e5e065b0faed8; r3.inc 74M against 300M): a test's tick count is not its own work. Hypothesis [H]: the count, like seon.profile's :calls (seon.profile/a-measures-calls-are-its-own-threads, web-2's find), takes other threads' or fibers' work in the process (a shard's shared root client, core.async's pool, a Flow proc), so it moves with what else runs. Meanwhile :seon.config.proofs/ticks-decide is false: no test is bounded or red by its ticks; every verdict still records them. Fix at the cause: the count a test is judged by is the engine ticks of the test's own thread or fiber (Jolt's run-interruptible budget and seon.profile/measure! read the same per-fiber counter), then the switch is turned on again, the ceiling restated by arithmetic. Proof, red first: seon.proofs-test/a-tests-ticks-are-the-same-alone-and-beside-busy-threads: one test at one digest, run 5 times alone and 5 times beside threads doing contracted work, reports ticks within 10% (red today by the measurement above; not committed red, so main stays green, but run red first when the fix is built). Cause read at both ends (root agrees, [read] until the proof runs): seon.profile/measure! reads jolt.host/ticks-of; off a fiber that is the thread's whole count (Jolt fibers.ss jolt-ticks), and the drain loop runs other fibers' dispatches on that thread while a test waits; nested drains also overwrite the dispatch mark. The fix is Jolt's (deps: jolt.host/an-off-fiber-measure-counts-its-own-lineage: a fiber inherits the measure open at its spawn; a dispatch outside the open measure's lineage is foreign and subtracted). The definition (root, agreed): a test's ticks are its own process's work along its lineage; a peer root's or child process's work is that process's. What the switch also turns off [M, 07:15]: an agent's test child gets no tick budget, so a test waiting for good there is not ended as a test (seon.agent-test/a-test-that-waits-for-good-is-told-and-the-next-agent-goes-on answered no verdict with it off; it proves the bound with it on). Docs false until this lands, each corrected in this task's landing: docs/cost.md:3 (cost is work done, never wall: and the test's own, by this definition), docs/merging.md#a-tests-tick-budget, AGENTS.md section 3a.9 and section 5 Estimates are proofs, docs/principles.md section 6 (a test's tick budget as enforcement); the ceiling :seon.config.agent/test-ticks is restated by arithmetic, not percentiles (300M came from p99.5 with 1.2x headroom). More evidence [M, web-2, 2026-11-15]: seon.source-test/a-test-that-reaches-a-process-is-found-by-the-reach-walk (estimate 20M) ran 200,307,137 ticks in a shard of 24 namespaces (target/prove/20261115T082124-27365/shard-2.log) and 21,908,989 alone, green: 9x by what ran before it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-tests-ticks-are-the-same-alone-and-beside-busy-threads}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-new-config-key-reruns-only-the-tests-that-read-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured [M flow, result/b94de997, 2026-11-15]: adding ONE config key with its default reruns 1,670 of 2,150 tests (78%): a test that reads config is selected as a reader of the whole map, not of the keys it reads. It holds flow's rail land until green (root, 2026-11-15). PRIOR WORK: per-key selection exists and is the mechanism this fixes, not one beside it: seon.program/graph-index answers each test's :takes, every config key its reach reads (src/seon/program.clj:2296), and reach-indexed re-answers only the tests whose :reads or :takes hold a moved input key (src/seon/program.clj:2364, input-moves); a verdict's reads are keyed [:av :config/key k] (seon.proofs/reads-key, src/seon/proofs.clj:601-610), landing 12 slice 1 (de04cffecf). So the 78% come from a reach that takes more than the keys it reads; to find, both ends read, before any code: (1) a read whose key is not a literal (config/value of a computed key) taking every key, (2) the registry walkers (seon.program/registry-walkers: seon.config/keys-declared, schema-forms) whose readers take :seon.env/schemas, which the new key's schema entry moves, (3) the config map's own digest in the inputs. COST: the lower bound of a new key is the tests whose reach reads that key by name, 0 for a key nothing reads yet, plus the readers of the whole registry (the config check and the schema tests, tens, not 1,670); selection is O(readers of the moved keys) over the held index, ~3.3k ticks a seek. Proof, red first and red today: a branch that adds one key with its default, nothing reading it, selects only the registry's own readers by test-plan. CAUSE FOUND [M root result/d9d2d387, 2026-11-15 13:40, main's graph, 2,167 tests]: 1,570 tests take :seon.env/schemas, the whole schema class, through seon.program/env-class-readers' seon.db/entity-index (src/seon/program.clj:1629; also seon.reconcile/index and seon.merge/content-datoms, 1623-1624); a new config key's entry in resources/seon/schemas/seon.config*.edn moves that class (seon.fx/env-class, fx.clj:346), so 1,570 plus the process tests rerun. A config key's entry is no entity schema: entity-index chooses among entity schemas only. The fix is at the data: the class these readers take is the entity schemas (and, for reconcile/merge, the attributes), keyed per entry as landing 12's reads are ([:av :schema/key k]), so a config key's entry moves only its own readers; :takes p50 is 47 keys, max 210, and 2,112 tests take the same 8 config keys (the process tests' env), a separate finding for the same proof. THE 8 SHARED KEYS [M root result/c8fab6b9; src read]: 2,112 tests take the same keys (ai/script, ai/reasoning-effort, root/profile, source/gate-checkout, source/root, proofs/max-workers, proofs/max-load, ...) because a hand-written set in seon.config (since deleted: the config map's {:optional true} is the one statement) naming 11 keys, is read by closed (config.clj:111), which every config read reaches: the reach takes a key a form names, so every config reader takes all 11 though it reads none of their values. They need none of them: the set says which keys a config may lack, a property of each key, and its own docstring already names the home (each key's schema entry, task seon.db/one-definition-of-each-entity step 2). Fix: an :optional property on each key's schema entry, closed derives the set from the registry, the hand list is deleted (done 2026-11-16: the config map marks optional keys itself). COST of a land after both fixes [M git log main 2026-11-15, 40 lands]: 11 of 40 changed a schema file and each moved >= 1,570 tests by the whole class; after the fix a key entry's edit moves its own readers, and an entity schema's edit still moves entity-index's readers (~1,150: entity-index really reads every entity schema to choose one), so the cut is on key-entry edits only; the 29 lands with no schema change move as many tests as before. Which of the 11 changed only key entries is to be measured from their diffs before the build. Reviews: data (seon.db/entity-index, the per-entry class), deps (the per-entry seek)."
