 {:seon.task/name "a-test-forks-from-the-standing-system-and-a-binary-test-reruns-only-for-its-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "FOCUS (docs/plan.md THE FOCUS, the one reference). Done: an agent edits on its branch; seon_prove runs only the tests its change reaches, each in a fork of its branch's warm process, reusing every result whose reads did not move, from any branch; seon_done is a land request on main answered once. No bin/land, bin/prove, bin/test for sessions, fast lane, land count or names file. Every run of this session goes through seon_prove; what stops it is fixed first. DESIGN, steps 1-3 (tests leads; root reviews). (A) A test's result is a fact {test, reads (one value: its read keys), reads-digest, status, failures with their data (a wall-spent's dump), ticks, bytes}, identity (test, reads-digest, status) (data agreed: two branches never conflict; flaky is a query: two statuses at one reads-digest). (B) Read keys at datom level, keyed by content: a function by [:ea form :form/code], a schema entry [:ea e :schema/form], a config key [:ea e :config/form] (root's :config/value is no attribute: facts.edn :config/form), a file [:ea e :file/text]; a key no entity holds [:av k v] so its arrival moves it. LANDED c5d26728e8 (red first: an [:av] key's content was the entity id, so an edited value was reusable). (C) Selection = what the change can have moved, O(d): (i) tests whose reach digest moved (code: the program's own digests, as today) and (ii) the readers of the change's datoms: seon.db/readers over an index of each kept result's read keys (reads-add), the index a derived value the prove proc builds once from the results it holds and updates as each result is written (never rebuilt per plan). Then the reach digest stops taking :seon.env/schemas and config whole: a schema or config edit selects exactly the results that read the moved entries, so one key edit reruns its readers, not ~1,600 (today's fan-out). Correctness needs (ii): without it a schema edit would move no digest and a stored result would be reused unread. (C) STATUS: slice 1 LANDED dc0c9be7a3 (readers of moved datoms selected; newest-green index held and advanced, 669M once). Slice 2 (the reach digest without whole :seon.env/schemas) needs a result's reads to cover the schema entries its writes were checked against [M result/bbee7837]: data's seon.db/the-checked-write-records-the-schema-entries-it-checked records the schema KEYS a checked write was judged by (a seek in the write's db would name the test world's entity, which matches no branch change: most tests write into their own world), and tests' half: the runner binds that record around each test and verdict-tx maps each key to the branch's [:ea e :schema/form], as read-keys does for a static schema row (today read-keys is the static closure only). Then a one-entry schema edit selects 0-737 tests, not 1,654 [result/aa6fb73a]. (D) DROPPED by measurement [M root result/bf75869c, 300 tests]: trying each distinct read set of a test's greens (13.7 a test on average) reuses 2 more than the newest alone (0.7%), at 12.5B ticks to evaluate; reuse stays the newest green, as landed. Its design, kept for the record: Reuse for a selected test t: one AVET seek of t's results, the distinct read sets among them (measured: of 300 tests' two newest passes, 197 read the same set [result/c9d51972]; so one or two sets a test), each set digested on this branch's value (reads-digests, each distinct key read once), then a seek (t, reads-digest): a hit is reused. Root's newest-only proposal misses another branch's result whose reads differ from the newest (its own gap 1); the distinct sets close it at the cost of the few sets. (E) Where results live (step 3): on the branch whose run wrote them, merged into main with it, read by seon.agent/results-at [db tests] from the branch's own value (it holds main's after main is merged in). Until (3) lands they stay on :main/results, and seon.agent/results-db reads it from the planned db's store, so root's plan reuses too (LANDING: d9902699d9; root's proposal to drop root's results db is right once (3) lands, not before: root plans main today and read nothing). (E) FILE-LEVEL PLAN (tests, for root's and data's read before code): one writer per branch, so results are written by the process that owns the branch: an agent's run writes its results as :seon.proof facts on its own branch, in its run's commit (seon.agent's record-results!, which writes :seon.test/* facts there today, writes verdict-tx's facts instead; the :seon.test/* facts and their readers go: render.ui's agent-state and proof-story, ctx.status, seon.agent's judge reads, in the same land or the next, each on results-at); main's run's results are written on main by root's writer (the prove proc's verdicts-write targets the run's branch when it is main, never another). results-at and results-db read db itself (the branch holds main's after main merges in); data's one :merge carries :seon.proof entities (content identity, so two branches never conflict) into main with the branch. Deleted: :main/results (after its newest greens are carried to main once), verdicts-write's results-branch and its clone-branch step, :seon.config.proofs/results-branch, seon.agent/results-db's branch name, the :seon.test/* facts. Main's size: data's collection keeps each test's newest results. Needs data: the merge carries :seon.proof/* (and confirm one writer per branch holds for the agent's run commit). COST: a run's results one commit on its own branch, as today's facts are; results-at one seek a test on db. (E) STATUS 2026-11-16 13:5x: step 1 LANDED 20ec01d401 (identity is content: test, reads digest, status). 2a LANDING (an agent's run writes verdict-tx facts on its own branch; own-results-first reads the branch then :main/results; the child's :schema-reads fix). NEXT: 2b moves every :seon.test/* reader (render.ui agent-state and proof-story, ctx.status, the judge's reads, gate-tx) onto results-at; 2c deletes :seon.test/*, :main/results, verdicts-write's results branch and clone step, :seon.config.proofs/results-branch, and fixes :seon.proof/id's schema description (a schema-file change: batch it into one restart land); read-moved then reads the branch's index. ALSO OPEN: (D) dropped by measurement; ticks-decide turns on when tests fork one per test (step 2, runtime's); seon_done should hand reached process tests to the gate as a message (web/context, sent). (F) Interim: lands name test-plan's :run (the one-eval form, AGENTS §3); seon_prove calls the same plan and the names file goes in step 5. ESTIMATES (lower bound per plan of n candidates, d moved datoms): selection (i) the graph update O(changed forms), 12-22M ticks [M deps]; (ii) readers: d datoms x 5 keys, one map lookup each, ~1k ticks a datom, plus the index's upkeep one reads-add per result written (~410 keys, ~50k ticks); reuse (D): n x (one seek ~10k + one value read ~50k) + reads-digests over the distinct keys (today 0.97B for 2,268 tests, the digest's own task halves it); today's measured whole plan at root 1.57B with reuse [result/ce6c9d2b]. Proofs, red first: seon.proofs-test/a-schema-entry-edit-reruns-its-readers-only (C), seon.proofs-test/a-result-of-another-branch-whose-reads-hold-is-reused (D). OLD CHECKLIST (removed; its evidence stays below). AUDIT 2026-11-16 (owner: find the existing Seon mechanism first; never a duplicate; where ours is worse, one hybrid that serves Seon agents and sessions). Today's work against what Seon has: (A) seon.test-store/ask is a second socket client of the server beside seon.server/ask-streamed (server.clj ~579, typed :seon.server/unanswered, used by bin/prove --root and the relay); today's ask-bound-ms extended the duplicate, and its bound is a clock on our own system (docs/clocks.md#two-kinds-of-wait: such a wait ends on the producer's answer or death). Hybrid: ts/ask calls ask-streamed, so tests, sessions and agents share one client; the root names its own stall (``seon.system/stall-state``, recorded by the root's stall task) instead of a client timer; the runtime rail then removes the socket from runtime tests. (B) Step 1 summed costs from SHARD lines in bin/land's shell; :prove already streams each result's ticks (prove.clj ~643) to seon.main/prove-client. Hybrid: the :prove answer carries seon.proofs/own-cost of what it ran and prove-client prints it; the SHARD line and the awk go with bin/test's shard path (piece 6). (C) Step 2's seon.db/results-moved became a scan; the read-key index exists (seon.db/reads-add, seon.db/readers, plan primitive 2). Fix in held step 2: results-moved is readers over an index of the results' reads values. (D) seon.proofs/*test-started-ns* is a second notion of a test's deadline beside seon.agent/arm-wall! (agent.clj ~1273, the one timer); goes with (A). (E) Replies went through Claude cross-session messages beside Seon messaging (bin/mcp call message): Seon messaging only. (F) A notes file held state: deleted; state is this task. (G) The hang was read from target/results files because bin/test writes only files (gap 2, piece 6). (H) A task was cancelled with raw datoms instead of my.root/cancel! (same datom here; the 127 slow-test tasks go through cancel!). (I) Task entries were edited as files, not on the agent's branch: seon.repl/edit! does not serve task entries yet (context's kind 7). (J) Lands were awaited by a ps loop: the by-hand count step 6 deletes. EVIDENCE: CHECKLIST to the owner's ruling (decisions.md 2026-11-16: the test system fully inside Seon, no more hacks), in order; each step lands only when its red-first proof was seen red and is green, and says what it deletes. (1) The fast lane's line sums each test's own cost (seon.proofs/the-fast-lane-line-sums-each-tests-own-cost; proof seon.proofs-test/the-fast-lane-line-sums-each-tests-own-cost): the measure the later steps are judged by; deletes run_cost's grep. (2) Read keys as one value a result: DONE with no schema change (proof seon.proofs-test/a-verdicts-read-keys-are-one-value): verdict-tx writes no per-key :seon.proof/read-keys; reuse takes the newest written passing result (its AVET :tx) and its :seon.proof/reads value; results-moved is seon.db/readers over the reads values; plan reuse at root 6.6B -> 1.57B ticks, same 1,486 reused [M result/ce6c9d2b]. The old datoms go with data's collection; the schema entry at a later restart. Reuse's low hit rate is a separate measured cause: read keys name whole form entities and :form/keywords moves them [result/be937ff1], open with root and data. (3) Process tests have main verdicts (seon.agent/a-process-tests-verdict-does-not-depend-on-its-batch rides here; proof seon.prove-test/a-main-run-writes-its-process-tests-verdicts): main's run plans its process tests as items (seon.agent/process-plan, mode :main) and the prove proc writes their :live verdicts; deletes the judge's :unknown hold for a process test main never ran. (4) Tests are thin clients of :prove (proof seon.proofs-test/bin-test-is-a-thin-client-of-prove): bin/test asks the standing system's :prove (bin/mcp call prove) for named tests on its checkout (seon.system/prove-tree!), its tests forked from the standing system; deletes test_runner's shard path, the shared test roots (seon.test-store/shared-root!, :seon.config.root/shared), and the file verdict cache's writes. (5) bin/prove is a thin client too (seon.prove/the-shell-runner-is-deleted; proof seon.proofs-test/bin-prove-is-a-thin-client-of-prove): deletes bin/prove --out, the shell runner and its seed. (6) One land queue as data (proof seon.system-test/a-land-is-a-queued-request-answered-once): a land is a request entity on main that the system orders, proves through :prove and merges once; deletes bin/land's land count and race rules and the random wait. Its pre-land check answers ```seon.system/restart-for``` whole, not ```seon.fx/runtime-for``` alone: 13a1e8fef9 restarted root on restart-for's tree clause while runtime-for said :keeps (runtime fixed that divergence in 09e83a2d59). (7) Every land through :prove and seon_done (proof seon.system-test/a-sessions-branch-lands-through-seon-done): a session's work merges as a Seon agent's does; deletes bin/land --fast, bin/land and bin/prove, and AGENTS.md's fast-lane section with them. Speed targets stay this task's acceptance (below). EVIDENCE AND EARLIER PLAN: TESTS AREA STATE AND ORDER (2026-11-16, written for a fresh session with no notes): landed on main: no-verdicts never read as reds (f4ca2f3a4c, 29dca89515, 3299ca7589); named main asks wait for the tests branch's runner and runners end with their last item (7f4e842206, 0b667d45c0); reuse by reads at each test's newest green with keys read once (3299ca7589: 88.8B -> 5.2B ticks for main's 2,258 tests at root); seon.test-store/ask bounded and typed with the root's stall-state (13a1e8fef9); bin/land's ticks print (ff272aae49, its sums wrong). Next, in order: (0) seon.proofs/the-fast-lane-line-sums-each-tests-own-cost; (1) seon.proofs/a-verdict-is-an-entity-that-names-its-environment step 2 (read keys one value a verdict: a schema change that restarts root, so ask root first and say so in the land message); (2) seon.system/a-trees-prove-in-a-test-root-always-answers; (3) seon.proofs/a-land-runs-every-name-it-is-given; (4) seon.prove/a-named-ask-on-main-waits-on-mains-run-never-refused's join and seon.prove/an-asker-that-stops-waiting-tells-its-owner; (5) seon.agent/a-process-tests-verdict-does-not-depend-on-its-batch; then this task. Measures to start from [M at root, 2026-11-16]: the results branch held 21,715 verdicts for 2,259 tests (median 9 a test, max 36), 92% of its datoms :seon.proof/read-keys; a cold plan's reuse costs 5.2B ticks, 4.3B of it finding newest greens and reading their keys. Root's standing rule: ask root before any land that changes a schema or config. Root's spec for E, 2026-11-15 (the owner asked for a plan for both test classes, and that most updates trigger no system test); plan due to root after landing 12's cleanup land, reviewed by deps and runtime (seon.fx/runtime-